\begin{abstract}
Who pays for reasoning that an online AI service generates but never delivers? The answer depends on how its model firm and execution cloud divide revenue, computation costs, and the right to choose the number of candidate answers. A finite service game compares fixed capacity fees with revenue sharing under both model and cloud control. Four profit differences characterize when private choices provide fewer, the same, or more attempts than the welfare-maximizing choice. Pooling requests changes these choices through two channels. Lower incremental cost can make a cloud prefer more attempts. Lower cost levels can instead make the cloud accept a service it previously rejected, even when incremental cost is unchanged. Under the stated participation conditions, either channel can reduce welfare while lowering cost at every fixed attempt budget. A configuration-dependent extension separates cost savings from changes in token bills, and a contract-by-pooling comparison identifies their welfare interaction. The accompanying measurement design links these conditions to delivered-answer quality, token bills, latency, and resource use. Generation pilots establish five deployment envelopes and document residual truncation. Together, the analysis and measurement design show how upstream contracts transmit the incentives created by charging for non-delivered computation.
\end{abstract}
